    'nuances' => array(
        'INDUSTRY STYLE (native technical editor): Claim no more than the English proves: never add eşsiz, benzersiz, rakipsiz or mükemmel where the English has no such word. Keep the source\'s own units and never append a conversion it does not give (no "(889 mm)" after "35″"). One term per concept on a page: devir for rotor speed, debi for measured flow, kartuş for the turbo core. Break English chains: no "X, Y ve Z ile donatılmış olup" runs, no 3-deep possessive stacks, no filler "olarak".',
        'A MACHINE OR SYSTEM NAME IS REBUILT, NOT TRANSLATED WORD BY WORD: front every English qualifier that trails the head noun or hangs off "with", and never let it land on the neighbouring noun. "Vertical balancing machine standard" is "Standart dikey balans makinesi"; "Engine cooling fan with motor balancing machine" is "Motorlu motor soğutma fanı balans makinesi"; "Fan and motor balancing machine" is "Fan ve motor balans makinesi", never "Fan ve motorlu balans makinesi". Inside a NAME the head is "balans makinesi", never the process noun "balanslama makinesi". A card lead "<Brand> <Product> system for <use> applications." becomes "<Brand> <Product> sistemi, <use> uygulamalarına yöneliktir." ("fluid power rotary motion" = "akışkan gücü alanındaki döner hareket", never a bare noun stack) If the Turkish shows three -ı/-i/-u/-ü possessive heads in a row, rebuild it; render "a wide range of X" as "çok çeşitli X", never "geniş bir X yelpazesi"; and never gloss a word you already translated. A product name that trails its generic noun in English moves in front of it: "Newest measuring and control unit ROTORTEST" is "En yeni ROTORTEST ölçüm ve kontrol ünitesi".',
        'AN ENGLISH NOUN USED AS A MODIFIER TAKES -lI/-lU, NOT THE BARE STEM: "roll brake test system" is "rulolu fren test sistemi"; "half case RDU/PTU" is "yarım gövdeli RDU/PTU"; "residential appliances" is "konut tipi cihazlar". Parts and features take -lI on EVERY coordinated noun, never "...-e sahip": "Pedestals with integrated pins and V-blocks" is "Entegre pimli ve V-bloklu yatak ayakları", never "Entegre pimler ve V-blokları olan"; "su soğutmalı", "servo tahrikli"; keep "...olan" for one owned part ("kendi muylusu olan rotorlar"). Never use "sahip olmak" for credentials or abstractions: degrees are "almak" or "mezun olmak", opportunities "elde etmek", capabilities gained "kazanmak". Never reproduce an English hyphen chain word for word: "<X>-as-a-service" inverts to "hizmet olarak balanslama (BaaS)"; "end-of-line" is "hat sonu" with no genitive.',
        'A SPEC OR FEATURE BULLET IS A NOUN PHRASE, AND AN ADJECTIVE STAYS AN ADJECTIVE: "Check relief pressure" in a check list is "Tahliye basıncı kontrolü", never "...kontrol edin"; "Easily add new models" is "Yeni modellerin kolayca eklenmesi"; "Compatible with most common leak detectors" ends in "...dedektörleriyle uyumlu". A description of what we deliver never becomes an imperative: "Periodic verification of machine operations" is "Makine çalışmasının periyodik doğrulaması". "Interfaces with X" is "X ile entegre çalışır". A compliance bullet puts the standard first and ends in "uygun": "Safety norms ISO 21940-23:2012 compliant" is "ISO 21940-23:2012 emniyet normlarına uygun"; a directive becomes "Makine Emniyeti Yönetmeliğine (2006/42/AT) uygun" (EC = AT). In a "<measure> with <configuration>" row the configuration is a condition, never a second noun: "Maximum distance between bearers with standard bed" is "Standart kızakta yatak mesnetleri arasındaki maksimum mesafe", never "Standart kızakla ... arasındaki" (= between the bed and the bearers); "with/without belt drive" is "kayış tahrikiyle/kayış tahriki olmadan". Keep one shape across a sibling list.',
        'PRESS HEADLINES ARE SENTENCE CASE WITH A SUBJECT COMMA, BUT THE JOB TITLE INSIDE IS CAPITALISED WORD BY WORD, its division or scope included, scope before rank, no comma: "Hires Jack Pennuto as Executive Vice President, Aerospace & Industrials" is "Jack Pennuto\'yu Havacılık-Uzay ve Sanayi İcra Başkan Yardımcısı olarak işe aldı". A functional scope is plural before "-dAn Sorumlu": "Küresel Üretim Operasyonlarından Sorumlu Kıdemli Başkan Yardımcısı"; "VP of Sales for Transportation" is "Ulaştırma Satışlarından Sorumlu Başkan Yardımcısı". A -dAn Sorumlu title that also names a unit puts the unit last in parentheses: "VP of Global Sales, Aerospace & Industrials" is "Küresel Satışlardan Sorumlu Başkan Yardımcısı (Havacılık-Uzay ve Sanayi)"; if the title already ends in "(CCO)" or another acronym, never add a second bracket: the scope goes in front, "Ulaştırma ile Test & Measurement Systems Ticari İşlerden Sorumlu Üst Yöneticisi (CCO)". The business unit Aerospace & Industrials is a proper name, "Havacılık-Uzay ve Sanayi", capitalised the same in titles, headlines, tabs and chips. Seniority is exact: Senior VP = "Kıdemli Başkan Yardımcısı", VP = "Başkan Yardımcısı", Executive VP = "İcra Başkan Yardımcısı", a Chief X Officer keeps C-level rank: CCO = "Ticari İşlerden Sorumlu Üst Yönetici (CCO)", but CTO = "Teknoloji Direktörü (CTO)" with any scope in front and the case on the last word: "Promotes X to CTO of Transportation" is "X\'i Ulaştırma Teknoloji Direktörlüğüne terfi ettirdi". Published = "Yayımlandı" (yayımlamak, never yayınlamak = broadcast). Press verbs keep the fact: Hires = "işe aldı", Appoints/Names = "atadı", Welcomes = "ekibine kattı", Joins = "katıldı", Promotes = "...-e terfi ettirdi"; "Announces Erin Dillard as Executive Vice President of Ascential Care" is "Erin Dillard\'ın Ascential Care İcra Başkan Yardımcılığına getirildiğini duyurdu", never "Erin Dillard\'ı ... olarak duyurdu". Keep the dateline as the source writes it ("Shelby Township, Mich.,") with a long date ("25 Ağustos 2026"); the appositive "Ascential Technologies, a leading global provider of X, ..." becomes "X alanında önde gelen küresel sağlayıcı olan Ascential Technologies, ...", never "...sağlayıcısı olarak"; the word after the dateline dash starts the sentence, so capitalise it in every dateline: "Mich., 22 Eylül 2026 — Otomatik ...".',
        'NUMBER, DATE AND UNIT CONVENTIONS ARE ONE PER DOCUMENT: the decimal separator is a comma in every figure, formula and tolerance ("1,5 milyon", "0,0065 mm", "9,81"); the thousands separator is a period from five digits up ("13.000") while a four-digit technical figure may stay unseparated ("2500 d/dk"); currency follows the number ("1.500 TL"); time is 24-hour; a form date is DD.MM.YYYY but a press date is long ("25 Ağustos 2026"). Per-minute units are Turkish - l/dk, kg/dk - and RPM is "d/dk", never "d/d" or "rpm". A space separates every number from its unit symbol ("50-60 Hz", "400 V", "16 A", "350 bar"; only "%100" is closed up), a span takes a plain hyphen ("6-10 kat"). Never keep "@" in Turkish: "60 runs @ 95%" is "60 çalıştırmada %95". Never put -lAr on a noun after a number or in a stat counter: "13.000 proje", "40 tesis"; "and counting" joins with ve and names its subject: "13.000 proje ve sayı artmaya devam ediyor".',
        'COMPANY BOILERPLATE IS REWRITTEN IN TURKISH BUSINESS IDIOM, THEN REUSED CHARACTER FOR CHARACTER ON EVERY PAGE THAT CARRIES IT: "global footprint" is "küresel faaliyet ağı"; "the expertise of 2,300 professionals across 40 locations" is "40 tesisteki 2.300 profesyonelin bilgi birikimiyle"; "the company\'s uniqueness lies in" is "Şirketi benzersiz kılan"; "Backed by the brands you trust. Powered by the quality you expect." is "Güvendiğiniz markaların desteğiyle. Beklediğiniz kalitenin gücüyle." An English passive slogan becomes active Turkish: "We\'re driven by purpose and powered by expertise." is "Bizi amacımız yönlendirir, gücümüzü uzmanlığımızdan alırız." Take the Turkish pairing over the dictionary word: sharp minds = "parlak zihinler", deep expertise = "engin uzmanlık", make ideas real = "hayata geçirmek", "our many brands, like Burke Porter" = "Burke Porter gibi pek çok markamız" (never "örneğin" mid-phrase). What we do to a machine: designed or engineered = "tasarlanmıştır" (never the calque "mühendislik edilmiştir"), built or manufactured = "üretilmiştir"; "geliştirilmiştir" only where the source says developed; never "inşa etmek" for a machine. A machine fault is "arıza", a human assembly mistake "hata".',
        'A TITLE TAIL, A CHIP LIST AND QUOTED SPEECH EACH HAVE ONE FIXED SHAPE. In "<Descriptor> - Ascential Test & Measurement" translate only the descriptor, in sentence case, and leave the brand tail in English: "Axle Balancers - Ascential Test & Measurement" is "Aks balans makineleri - Ascential Test & Measurement", never "Axle Balancers - Ascential Test ve Ölçüm". In a comma-separated taxonomy chip list every chip matches its enforced term exactly: "Balancing, Refrigeration & Gas Management, Aerospace & Industrials" is "Balanslama, Soğutma ve gaz yönetimi, Havacılık-Uzay ve Sanayi". Quoted speech and a quoted nickname keep “ ” (“Alessandro, proje...”, Zhigang “Manfred” Zhuang), never straight quotes; a suffix on a proper noun takes one straight ASCII apostrophe ("Ascential\'in"). "&" between two words is "ve" in every label, including a fragment that ends in it: "Service &amp;" is "Servis ve". In a "<Category> - <Subtype>" label the common noun after the hyphen is lower case: "Yatay manuel - su sayaçları", "Yatay manuel - üniversal". A clause that introduces an inserted phone number, email or link ends with a colon, and "at <number>" is "şu numaradan", never "şuradan": "You can phone us at" is "Bizi şu numaradan arayabilirsiniz:".',
        'CONSISTENCY, THE LARGEST DEFECT CLASS: one English term or label gets exactly ONE Turkish rendering on every surface, <title> and meta included.',
