        'A BRAND, PRODUCT, DIVISION OR MODEL NAME IS NEVER TRANSLATED, DECLINED AWAY OR HALF-TRANSLATED, in any surface: XL Machine, ADAS Garage, Cimat, Galileo, Kleinknecht, Lismar, Titan, Burke Porter, Universal Balancing, WinterPark Engineering, Bauer, Sentia, Ascential Care, Ascentialytics, Ascential Medical & Life Sciences, and model codes such as CMT-TR, CMT-VSR, LGC30XX, UNI-64. A legal name keeps every word ("Engineering" too); translate only the generic head noun around it ("Ascential Medical & Life Sciences bölümü"). Model codes and acronyms keep their capitals: "HC DS02", not "hc ds02". A trademarked English phrase keeps its TM on the English, never on a Turkish paraphrase.',
        'TRANSLATE A HEADING SPLIT ACROSS TWO LINES, SPANS OR SEGMENTS AS ONE TURKISH PHRASE, resolving the compound term first and putting the single Turkish word in the accented span: "VERTICAL / balancing machines" is "DİKEY balans makineleri"; "EDDY / current technology" is "girdap akımı teknolojisi". A split "Our" or "Your" becomes a suffix on the noun and its span stays empty: "<span>Our</span> <span>results</span>" is "<span></span> <span>Sonuçlarımız</span>", never "Bizim" or "Sizin". Never mirror English order around the tag: "Service on <span>every continent.</span>" is "Hizmetimiz <span>her kıtada.</span>".',
        'TURKISH NOUN COMPOUNDS TAKE THE POSSESSIVE ON THE HEAD NOUN (izafet): "landing gear test" is "iniş takımı testi", "metals automation" is "metal otomasyonu", "Paper & Plastic Industry" is "Kâğıt ve plastik sanayii". A label ending in a bare "test", "otomasyon", "montaj" or "sanayi" after a modifying noun is ungrammatical; where compounds would stack, use "için": "Automotive Industry Balancers" is "Otomotiv sanayii için balans makineleri". Do not strand a possessive on a coordinated item that is not the head: "yedek parça ve analitik", not "...analitiği".',
        'CHECK THE CASE THE MAIN VERB LICENSES BEFORE FINISHING THE CLAUSE, AND PUT IT ON EVERY COORDINATED OBJECT: etkilemek, artırmak, azaltmak, iyileştirmek all take the accusative, so "süresini, onarımları ve daha pek çok alanı etkileyebilir" - no item may drift into the locative ("alanda"), which leaves a transitive verb with no object.',
        'PUT THE SUBJECT OF A NOMINALISED -DIK/-(y)EcEK CLAUSE IN THE GENITIVE: "başkalarının sınır gördüğü yerde", "ekiplerin doğruladığı veri", "dünyanın ilerlediğine" - never "başkaları sınır gördüğü yerde". Apply it to every such clause. Leave the subject nominative only in temporal -DIğInDA adverbials ("insanlar güce kavuştuğunda").',
        'IN "help someone do X" THE POSSESSIVE ON THE VERBAL NOUN GOES ON THE CUSTOMER AND THE VERBAL NOUN TAKES THE DATIVE: "hangi zorlukları çözmenize yardımcı olabiliriz?", never "çözmede size yardımcı olabiliriz" and never "-memize", which reverses who acts.',
        'PRESERVE THE SUBJECT AND THE AGENT OF THE ENGLISH SENTENCE, AND BACK-TRANSLATE ANY TWO-PARTICIPANT CLAUSE BEFORE MOVING ON: "understand how our site is used" is "sitemizin nasıl kullanıldığını anlamak", not "sitenizi nasıl kullandığımızı". A company-name subject beside "our" takes first person: "XL Machine provides value through our services" is "XL Machine olarak hizmetlerimizle değer sunuyoruz"; "At CIMAT, we offer" is "CIMAT olarak ... sunuyoruz", never "CIMAT\'ta". Never insert a comitative -le/-la that turns the company\'s own brands into third-party partners.',
        'CORRELATIVES (ister...ister, hem...hem, ne...ne, gerek...gerek) MUST CARRY THE PARTICLE ON EVERY BRANCH, WITH EVERY BRANCH IN THE SAME SHAPE AND THE SHARED VERB AFTER THE LAST ONE: "İster sıfırdan başlayalım ister mevcut teknolojilerden yola çıkalım, ...". Never pair a verb-bearing branch with a bare noun or postpositional phrase, and never mix "ister" with "veya" or "ya da".',
        'ONE ADDITIVE MARKER AND ONE VERB ROOT PER SENTENCE: never "Ayrıca ... de" together, never "de" in two adjacent clauses, never the same root twice ("kontrol sistemi tarafından yönetilir", not "...kontrol edilir"; "hızlı yanıt sağlar; bu sayede çabuk uyum gösteririz", not "...uyum sağlarız").',
        'NEVER OPEN A TURKISH SENTENCE WITH A COORDINATING CONJUNCTION - not "Ve", "Veya", "Ya da", "Ama" or "Fakat". English sentence-initial "And..." / "But..." becomes "Ayrıca...", "Bunun yanı sıra...", "Ancak..." placed inside the clause. Do not use "veya" twice at two syntactic levels in one clause: use "ya da" for the outer alternative and keep "veya" for the inner list.',
        'KEEP A SET OF SIBLING HEADINGS, BULLETS, TABS OR <select> OPTIONS PARALLEL TO EACH OTHER AND IN ONE CAPITALISATION PATTERN - Turkish sentence case: "Amacımız / Çalışma şeklimiz / Sonuçlarımız" are three noun phrases, so never let one card carry a finite verb clause ("Nasıl çalışıyoruz"). Options inside one group read top to bottom as one pattern: "Onarım ve saha/uzaktan servis", "Yedek parça ve sarf malzemeleri", "Öncelik 1: Yüksek öncelik".',
        'FIELD LABELS AND BUTTONS THAT START AN ACTION TAKE AN INDEFINITE, UNMARKED OBJECT: "Destek talebi gönderin", "Servis talebi oluşturun", "Teklif isteyin" - never the definite accusative ("Destek talebini gönderin"), which points at a request the visitor has not created.',
        'NEVER LEAVE AN ENGLISH WORD IN A DESCRIPTOR, ATTRIBUTE OR WIDGET: aria-label, title, alt, placeholder, consent and chat chrome are fully Turkish ("Ascential Technologies\'i takip edin", not "Follow Ascential Technologies"). Translate a descriptor around its acronym or brand-like words: "NDT inspection" is "tahribatsız muayene (NDT)", "SME" (subject matter expert) is "konu uzmanı", "PM" (preventive maintenance) is "önleyici bakım". An English common noun never takes a Turkish suffix: "Field Balancers" is "Saha balans cihazları", never "Field Balancer\'lar". Trade loanwords a Turkish engineer says out loud stay (HVAC, OBD, NVH, ADAS, dinamometre), but never beside a translated sibling: "HVAC fan & blower balancing" is "HVAC fan ve üfleyici balanslama".',
        'NEVER LEAVE A BARE ADJECTIVE AS A STANDALONE LINK, TAB OR LIST LABEL, and never strand an English adjective before a noun or question word: "Medical" as an affiliate link is "Medikal", never "Tıbbi"; a category chip "Electrical" is the noun "Elektrik", never "Elektriksel"; "Industrial" as a market or division label is "Sanayi", but before a machine or unit it is "endüstriyel" ("Industrial balancing machine" is "Endüstriyel balans makinesi", never "Sanayi tipi balans makinesi"); "Aerospace or Industrial challenges" is "havacılık veya sanayi alanındaki zorluklar".',
        'USE THE SHOP-FLOOR SENSE OF ANY WORD THAT HAS AN EVERYDAY HOMONYM: field service = "saha" (not "alan"); machine down = "arızalı"; "column" = "kolon"; throughput = "üretim hızı"; the efficiency of a machine, motor or pump = "verim" ("mekanik verim", "yüksek güç altında verim"), "verimlilik" only for productivity; sensitive to disturbance = "duyarlı", while "hassas" means precise; an accurate machine "yüksek doğrulukla çalışır", never "doğrudur"; embedded = "entegre" ("gömülü" for a part set into the machine body), never "hat içi" (= in-line); a machine guard = "muhafaza", not "koruma"; a machine bed = "kızak" ("yatak" is only a bearing); machine or standard safety = "emniyet"; capabilities of a machine or test stand = "kabiliyetler" ("ek test kabiliyetleriyle"), but the company\'s own Capabilities (nav, section heading) = "Yetkinlikler"; product line = "ürün yelpazesi"; tooling = "takım ve aparat imalatı"; "when people are empowered" is a clause, "insanlar ... güce kavuştuğunda", never the participle "güç verilmiş" or "güçlendirilmiş"; actionable information = "eyleme dönüştürülebilir bilgi", never "uygulanabilir"; available = "sunulur", never "mevcuttur"; drive a pump or shaft = "tahrik etmek" (not "sürmek"); a roller on a paper, conveyor or mill line = "merdane", on a brake or chassis tester = "rulo"; a field or service agent is one person, "temsilci", never "ekip"; cost of failure = "hatanın bedeli"; inquiry or support case = "talep"; asset on a service form = "ekipman"; an offering = "hizmet" or "çözüm" (not "sunum"); a content hub or a bare "Hub" nav label = "Merkez", a wheel hub = "poyra".',
        'STATUTORY AND LEGAL PAGE NAMES HAVE FIXED TURKISH EQUIVALENTS, NOT LITERAL ONES, AND A DEFINED TERM IS A FROZEN STRING: Imprint/Impressum = "Künye" (never "Yasal uyarı"); Terms = "Kullanım koşulları" (never bare "Koşullar"); Privacy Policy = "Gizlilik politikası"; Cookie Policy = "Çerez politikası". Declare a defined term once and repeat it character for character; "Name:" is "Ad Soyad:" and "Date:" is "Tarih:". Never drop or renumber a clause number.',
        'AN ADVERB OR ADJECTIVE THAT DESCRIBES HOW WE WORK ATTACHES TO THE VERB, NOT TO AN INANIMATE NOUN: "allowing us to adapt swiftly" is "hızla uyum sağlamamıza olanak tanır". Never add an adverb the English lacks, least of all one repeating an adjective you kept: "uncompromising possibilities" is "taviz vermeyen olanaklar", never "ödün vermeden ... taviz vermeyen". Adverbials stay by the verb: "baskı altında mevcut bir çözümü optimize etmek", never "baskı altındaki bir çözüm". When the subject changes inside a sentence, restate it: "merkezlerin işleme alanı 18″ x 30″ ölçülerine kadardır; bu merkezler +/- 0,002 mm\'ye kadar tolerans sağlayabilir"; after a thing-subject ("platformumuz"), a "we" verb needs its first-person ending: "bu sayede çabuk uyum gösterebiliyoruz". Every clause of a "we" sentence carries -Iz/-yoruz, never a bare third-person aorist: "Therefore, we incorporate the latest technology and deliver" is "Bu nedenle en son teknolojiyi kullanıyor, ... sunuyoruz", never "...teknolojiyi uygular;".',
        'TURKISH HAS DOTTED İ/i AND DOTLESS I/ı AS SEPARATE LETTERS: the uppercase of "i" is "İ" and of "ı" is "I". Capitalise the first character of every sentence, heading and CTA; but a fragment whose English starts lower case continues a sentence, so it stays lower case and keeps its connector: "tool" is "araç", "or select from the forms below and we\'ll reply" is "ya da aşağıdaki formlardan birini seçin, size yanıt verelim" (seçmek needs its object; imperative + optative join with a comma).',
        'ATTACH SUFFIXES TO FOREIGN PROPER NOUNS WITH ONE STRAIGHT ASCII APOSTROPHE, HARMONISED TO THE LAST PRONOUNCED VOWEL (the question particle too: "Life Sciences mi"), with -n-/-y- after a written final vowel: "Ascential\'in", "Sentia\'ya", "Moretti\'yi"; an English -er is said -ır ("Todd Bonner\'ı", "Jerome Stiger\'ı"), but a German name keeps its German vowel, ue = ü: "Uwe Krueger\'i", never "Krueger\'ı"; only a silent final consonant after a vowel sound adds the buffer ("Drew\'yu", "Michalet\'yi"). Never an apostrophe on a Turkish common noun or law name ("Yönetmeliğine"). The same name never appears with two different suffixes or two apostrophe styles on one page, and the same English headline or UI template produces exactly one Turkish rendering wherever it is reused ("Show N subcategories under X" is always "X altındaki N alt kategoriyi göster"). A headline object with a possessor is marked: "Announces New Division to..." is "...yeni bölümünü duyurdu". "<Nouns> from <Company>" puts the source first, never after a colon: "Ascential\'den yönetici atamaları ve şirket haberleri". The suffix consonant follows the last letter as a Turkish reader says it: after l, m, n, r, a vowel it is d/g ("Ascential\'de"); after p, ç, t, k, f, s, ş, h it is t/k, final -s included ("Systems\'ten", "Kleinknecht\'ten").',
        'A CARD EXCERPT CUT OFF MID-CLAUSE ENDS ON TURKISH THAT STANDS ON ITS OWN, THEN "…": finish the last full clause and carry the cut English tail over as a connector, never as a bare converb, participle, "için" with no object, or a placeholder pronoun: keep a trailing "from" as a -dAn başlayan phrase: "...conveyors and rollers, from…" is "...merdaneler için, …\'den başlayan özel balans makineleri…", never dropped. "…solutions combining X with Y, enabling…" is "…birleştiren çözümler; bu sayede…"; translate every word up to the ellipsis: "…applications. Designed…" is "…uygulamalarına yöneliktir. Tasarlandığı…"; a cut participle keeps Turkish order behind a leading ellipsis: "Custom test station designed…" is "…için tasarlanmış özel test istasyonu…". A text split around a link must read as one sentence with a finite verb once joined.',
    ),
